  "title": "Arjun Panditrao Khotkar v. Kailash Kushanrao Gorantyal",
  "jurisdiction": "India",
  "content": {
    "caseIdentity": "Arjun Panditrao Khotkar v. Kailash Kushanrao Gorantyal, (2020) 7 SCC 1",
    "court": "Supreme Court of India",
    "decisionYear": 2020,
    "citation": "(2020) 7 SCC 1",
    "facts": "The case concerned the proof and certification of electronic records tendered in election litigation.",
    "questionsBeforeCourt": [
      "Whether the statutory certificate requirement for computer outputs is mandatory and when secondary electronic evidence may be received.",
      "Whether the requirement of a certificate under Section 65B(4) [now BSA s. 63(4)] is mandatory, and whether the contrary view in Shafhi Mohammad dispensing with the certificate for parties not in possession was good law."
    ],
    "ratioDecidendi": "The certificate requirement is a condition precedent for admissibility of the relevant electronic record output, subject to the statutory route and the Court's power to facilitate production where the facts justify it.",
    "holding": "Held that compliance with the statutory certificate for electronic records is mandatory and an indispensable condition precedent for the admissibility of digital evidence.",
    "relevance": "Mandatory benchmark for all card skimming, paper leak, and cyber evidence tendered under Section 112 BNS.",
